00:49:23.140 --> 00:49:27.480
on people who accept all the cookies on their website.

1000
00:49:28.000 --> 00:49:30.280
She has a very funny line where she says,

1001
00:49:30.420 --> 00:49:34.260
you could say we get more information from the readers than they get from us.

1002
00:49:34.620 --> 00:49:35.140
Mm-hmm.

1003
00:49:35.320 --> 00:49:35.680
Mm-hmm.

1004
00:49:36.140 --> 00:49:39.780
Nicole is played by Ramona Young, and we got to interview her when we visited.

1005
00:49:39.780 --> 00:49:44.080
It got cut short because they needed her back on set, but you can listen to that interview.

1006
00:49:44.500 --> 00:49:44.760
Yeah.

1007
00:49:45.220 --> 00:49:48.220
Her and I found out we were both on The Real O'Neils together.

1008
00:49:48.500 --> 00:49:49.220
I know.

1009
00:49:49.480 --> 00:49:53.000
You were on multiple things together, but you never really met.

1010
00:49:53.280 --> 00:49:54.400
Never worked together.

1011
00:49:54.900 --> 00:49:56.620
That's like me and Rob Lowe.

1012
00:49:56.780 --> 00:49:58.740
We were on three projects together.

1013
00:49:59.280 --> 00:49:59.960
Hold up.

1014
00:50:00.080 --> 00:50:01.500
And we never were in a scene together.

1015
00:50:01.720 --> 00:50:07.740
Is there going to be a show where me and you and Rob Lowe and Ramona are all in one scene?

1016
00:50:07.980 --> 00:50:08.260
Yes.

1017
00:50:08.360 --> 00:50:09.440
On Mom Detectives.

1018
00:50:09.780 --> 00:50:10.520
Oh, my gosh.

1019
00:50:10.520 --> 00:50:11.480
Case number one.

1020
00:50:12.540 --> 00:50:12.940
Exactly.

1021
00:50:13.980 --> 00:50:14.660
All right.

1022
00:50:14.920 --> 00:50:18.980
Now we are going to meet Detrick, played by Melvin Gregg.

1023
00:50:19.160 --> 00:50:22.020
I'm so bummed we didn't get to interview Melvin.

1024
00:50:22.160 --> 00:50:22.420
I know.

1025
00:50:22.480 --> 00:50:24.220
We got to meet him, but we didn't get to interview him.

1026
00:50:24.260 --> 00:50:25.700
We got to chat with him on set.

1027
00:50:25.700 --> 00:50:26.860
Then they started rolling.

1028
00:50:26.860 --> 00:50:27.800
We had to whisk away.

1029
00:50:27.940 --> 00:50:29.020
And then he was in scenes.

1030
00:50:29.240 --> 00:50:29.500
Yes.

1031
00:50:29.860 --> 00:50:32.540
He explains that he sells ads for the paper.

1032
00:50:32.540 --> 00:50:39.600
And we also learned that he likes to cheer people up, specifically Nicole.

1033
00:50:41.140 --> 00:50:43.360
He's going to have a little crush on Nicole.

1034
00:50:43.620 --> 00:50:47.620
Angela, we have seen Nicole's desk and Detrick's desk.

1035
00:50:47.620 --> 00:50:50.280
Do you have a desk breakdown for us?

1036
00:50:50.540 --> 00:50:51.320
I do.

1037
00:50:51.600 --> 00:50:51.800
Okay.

1038
00:50:52.560 --> 00:50:56.700
So, Nicole's desk, now you're going to see this a little bit more later in the episode.

1039
00:50:57.060 --> 00:50:59.300
Right now, we've only seen a quick glimpse of it.

1040
00:50:59.300 --> 00:51:05.120
She has a framed picture of her dog, some type of like Stanley cup, you know, like water bottle.

1041
00:51:06.060 --> 00:51:10.560
Some little twinkly lights that it looks like she sort of strung around her partition.

1042
00:51:11.280 --> 00:51:19.440
The thing I'm most curious about is the birdcage that's got the little, you know, cloth covering it because the bird is clearly inside.

1043
00:51:19.620 --> 00:51:20.260
It's sleeping.

1044
00:51:20.440 --> 00:51:21.060
It's sleeping.

1045
00:51:21.080 --> 00:51:24.260
But yeah, she gets to bring her bird to work.

1046
00:51:24.260 --> 00:51:30.620
And this made me think of a gal I worked with at 1-800-Denis who would bring her parakeet, Dash, to work.

1047
00:51:30.840 --> 00:51:31.720
In a cage?

1048
00:51:32.300 --> 00:51:33.320
Obviously, right?

1049
00:51:33.480 --> 00:51:34.380
Not on her shoulder.

1050
00:51:35.060 --> 00:51:36.780
I don't know why I said in a cage.

1051
00:51:37.140 --> 00:51:41.780
Actually, she had this little travel, I don't even know if I'd call it a cage container.

1052
00:51:41.980 --> 00:51:43.000
It looked like a caboodle.

1053
00:51:43.100 --> 00:51:44.400
Remember the makeup caboodles?

1054
00:51:44.580 --> 00:51:44.800
Yeah.

1055
00:51:45.060 --> 00:51:48.220
It was like plastic, but the top was kind of open and graded.

1056
00:51:48.540 --> 00:51:52.420
And she could pop the lid open and they let Dash out.

1057
00:51:52.800 --> 00:51:53.540
It was okay.

1058
00:51:53.540 --> 00:51:54.860
At 1-800-Denis?

1059
00:51:54.900 --> 00:51:55.620
At 1-800-Denis.

1060
00:51:55.780 --> 00:51:58.740
And he had a ball that he loved so much.

1061
00:51:58.900 --> 00:52:00.800
It had a little bell inside it.

1062
00:52:00.940 --> 00:52:05.460
And if you called 1-800-Denis back in those days, you might have heard this in the background.

1063
00:52:05.640 --> 00:52:09.800
And that was Dash kicking his ball.

1064
00:52:09.980 --> 00:52:12.000
I had two parakeets growing up.

1065
00:52:12.680 --> 00:52:17.500
Pretty was the name of the first parakeet and Mango was the name of the second parakeet.

1066
00:52:17.840 --> 00:52:23.280
And Mango used to fly around our house and would sit on my shoulder when I played piano.

1067
00:52:24.060 --> 00:52:28.480
Dash would hang out at Paula, was the lady's name, in her little desk cubicle.

1068
00:52:28.740 --> 00:52:31.120
Or he would kick his ball down.

1069
00:52:31.360 --> 00:52:32.960
All of our desks were connected.

1070
00:52:33.020 --> 00:52:35.720
So you might have Dash just traveling down.

1071
00:52:36.120 --> 00:52:36.640
Wow.

1072
00:52:37.160 --> 00:52:37.340
Yeah.

1073
00:52:38.000 --> 00:52:40.140
I want more bird is all I'm saying.

1074
00:52:40.340 --> 00:52:41.640
Okay, I'm now intrigued.

1075
00:52:42.160 --> 00:52:43.940
We've seen it in the opening credits.

1076
00:52:44.300 --> 00:52:46.380
Now I see the cage on her desk.

1077
00:52:46.540 --> 00:52:47.760
When will we meet this bird?

1078
00:52:47.960 --> 00:52:48.800
When will we meet this bird?

1079
00:52:49.040 --> 00:52:49.340
Okay.

1080
00:52:49.760 --> 00:52:53.040
Melvin's desk, I will say, is a little bit more of a typical desk.

1081
00:52:53.220 --> 00:52:55.640
You know, papers, post-it notes, things like that.

1082
00:52:55.760 --> 00:52:56.820
Not too personalized.

1083
00:52:57.600 --> 00:53:00.200
Not super personalized, but oh my goodness.

1084
00:53:00.760 --> 00:53:03.440
Melvin, we need to discuss your computer screen.

1085
00:53:03.940 --> 00:53:05.620
He had so many tabs open.

1086
00:53:05.800 --> 00:53:07.900
Seriously, he has like 10 tabs open.

1087
00:53:08.920 --> 00:53:10.860
So he's got some digital clutter, lady.

1088
00:53:14.220 --> 00:53:19.520
There is one other desk that I think really rivals Esmeralda's for the amount of things.

1089
00:53:19.720 --> 00:53:20.960
And we'll get to it.

1090
00:53:21.460 --> 00:53:21.840
All right.

1091
00:53:22.060 --> 00:53:27.040
Well, speaking of Esmeralda, she's kind of flirting with the camera.

1092
00:53:27.700 --> 00:53:29.540
I loved this so much, lady.

1093
00:53:29.740 --> 00:53:31.720
This is my favorite moment of the episode.

1094
00:53:32.220 --> 00:53:33.960
Her acting is so brilliant.

1095
00:53:34.200 --> 00:53:37.840
Cassie and I were talking about it when I got here because this is her favorite moment.

1096
00:53:38.000 --> 00:53:38.760
Oh my gosh.

1097
00:53:38.920 --> 00:53:39.860
We've got to hear it.

1098
00:53:39.940 --> 00:53:40.500
Let's hear it.

1099
00:53:41.400 --> 00:53:47.400
You are going to get tired chasing my busy little buddy around with that camera.

1100
00:53:51.020 --> 00:53:55.640
Also, I'm a single mom, so there's lots going on in my life.

1101
00:53:57.040 --> 00:53:59.720
Anyway, here are two accountants and the head accountant.

1102
00:54:01.440 --> 00:54:04.180
Boring, boring, and head boring.

1103
00:54:05.680 --> 00:54:07.080
God, not again.

1104
00:54:07.760 --> 00:54:09.740
I'm not agreeing to any of this.

1105
00:54:10.000 --> 00:54:11.720
Don't you guys have enough after nine years?

1106
00:54:12.180 --> 00:54:13.320
Nobody wants this.

1107
00:54:13.540 --> 00:54:14.200
You know what?

1108
00:54:14.220 --> 00:54:16.740
You can't use my voice, my likeness, my face, nothing.

1109
00:54:19.640 --> 00:54:20.560
Oh my gosh.

1110
00:54:20.700 --> 00:54:21.700
This was so fun.

1111
00:54:21.820 --> 00:54:23.380
This is the reveal of Oscar.

1112
00:54:23.540 --> 00:54:28.480
Yes, but the turn that she takes leading there where she's like, flirty, flirty, and

1113
00:54:28.480 --> 00:54:29.700
then she's like, I'm a single mom.

1114
00:54:29.720 --> 00:54:30.740
I got a lot going on.

1115
00:54:30.900 --> 00:54:31.080
Yeah.

1116
00:54:31.360 --> 00:54:33.180
And then she kicks it over to accounting.

1117
00:54:33.780 --> 00:54:37.000
And then, yes, we discover Oscar.

1118
00:54:37.560 --> 00:54:38.680
Yeah, he runs away.

1119
00:54:38.880 --> 00:54:40.100
He's like, you heard it.

1120
00:54:40.200 --> 00:54:41.720
You cannot use my likeness, nothing.

1121
00:54:41.840 --> 00:54:42.720
I'm not doing this again.

1122
00:54:42.740 --> 00:54:44.640
And he runs in the bathroom and he slams the door.

1123
00:54:44.640 --> 00:54:50.920
And then the next thing we see is a title card with a message on it that says, yes,

1124
00:54:51.100 --> 00:54:51.540
we can.

1125
00:54:51.900 --> 00:54:55.680
There's no end date to the release Oscar signed in 2005.

1126
00:54:56.080 --> 00:54:56.360
Yes.

1127
00:54:56.460 --> 00:55:00.560
He's now a reluctant participant in this ongoing documentary.

1128
00:55:01.420 --> 00:55:03.300
He's got to be so pissed off.

1129
00:55:03.580 --> 00:55:05.580
Well, we're going to see this play out.

1130
00:55:05.980 --> 00:55:08.960
Like we said, we got to see the first four episodes of the show.

1131
00:55:08.960 --> 00:55:16.140
So, and without giving too much away, it's kind of a soft launch because he's going to

1132
00:55:16.140 --> 00:55:18.160
be avoiding the cameras.

1133
00:55:18.420 --> 00:55:19.720
He's going to get drawn in.

1134
00:55:19.840 --> 00:55:21.520
But I really loved that.

1135
00:55:21.620 --> 00:55:27.700
I love that we got to see this familiar face, but he's not in the main storylines at firSt.

1136
00:55:28.520 --> 00:55:31.060
He's going to get slowly drawn in.

1137
00:55:31.060 --> 00:55:31.260
Slowly pulled in.

1138
00:55:31.260 --> 00:55:36.420
I feel like we should mention the other members of this three-person boring accounting team

1139
00:55:36.420 --> 00:55:40.800
because they're played by two more of the paper's writer-performers,

1140
00:55:41.240 --> 00:55:43.820
Bimasola Ikumelo and Alex Edelman.

1141
00:55:43.980 --> 00:55:48.540
And they play Adalola and Adam, the other two accountants.

1142
00:55:49.100 --> 00:55:50.960
We're going to see Barry now.

1143
00:55:51.220 --> 00:55:56.500
Barry's desk area is sort of that classic desk of someone who has worked at the same company

1144
00:55:56.500 --> 00:55:58.780
for a long, long time.

1145
00:55:58.780 --> 00:56:02.460
It has clutter, but it is clutter that has earned its place.

1146
00:56:02.700 --> 00:56:07.460
For example, his desk is situated by a big column that's part of the building,

1147
00:56:07.460 --> 00:56:11.800
and he has covered that column with a whole bunch of little tiny newspaper clippings.

1148
00:56:12.040 --> 00:56:15.120
Yes, probably articles he wrote over the years, I would think.

1149
00:56:15.240 --> 00:56:15.800
We would hope.

1150
00:56:16.180 --> 00:56:22.880
Well, Mare is at her desk, and she's doing her business of pulling articles off the wire,

1151
00:56:22.900 --> 00:56:24.260
and she just says, you know what?

1152
00:56:24.260 --> 00:56:25.240
I need some air.

1153
00:56:25.720 --> 00:56:30.820
And we're going to now go outside, and we're going to see someone approaching the building,

1154
00:56:30.820 --> 00:56:34.780
but I think we should take a break, and when we come back, we'll tell you who it is.

1155
00:56:35.040 --> 00:56:37.280
Yes, we're going to meet a young man with purpose.

1156
00:56:38.040 --> 00:56:52.850
All right, we are back, and we are going to meet Ned Sampson.

1157
00:56:53.030 --> 00:56:55.890
He's this nice-looking young man.

1158
00:56:55.890 --> 00:56:57.530
He's got a backpack on.

1159
00:56:57.530 --> 00:57:01.630
He's played by Donal Gleason, double crossover connection.

1160
00:57:01.630 --> 00:57:04.130
He was in the miniseries The Patient with Steve Carell,

1161
00:57:04.290 --> 00:57:06.270
and the movie Fountain of Youth with John Krasinski.

1162
00:57:06.610 --> 00:57:07.830
How about that?

1163
00:57:08.130 --> 00:57:11.490
Well, we're going to learn, you know, he's super excited to be there.

1164
00:57:11.630 --> 00:57:15.510
It's his first day working at the Truth Teller, and he shares growing up,

1165
00:57:15.590 --> 00:57:17.170
he didn't idolize Superman.

1166
00:57:17.810 --> 00:57:23.190
He worshiped Clark Kent, who was saving the world by working at a newspaper.

1167
00:57:23.450 --> 00:57:25.510
Yes, he's very idealistic.

1168
00:57:25.690 --> 00:57:26.050
Yes.

1169
00:57:26.050 --> 00:57:32.190
And as he's talking, he kind of runs into Mare, who had gone—

1170
00:57:32.190 --> 00:57:33.090
Kind of runs in?

1171
00:57:33.250 --> 00:57:35.110
Yeah, he knocks her whole salad.

1172
00:57:35.390 --> 00:57:41.110
She's carrying this big salad, and he backs into her unknowingly with his enormous backpack,

1173
00:57:41.590 --> 00:57:44.910
knocking her salad out of her hands, dumping it all over the ground.

1174
00:57:44.950 --> 00:57:46.190
He never even sees her.

1175
00:57:46.470 --> 00:57:48.770
All I could think about was Hugh Grant in this moment.

1176
00:57:49.110 --> 00:57:49.630
Why?

1177
00:57:49.950 --> 00:57:51.610
He hates backpacks.

1178
00:57:52.010 --> 00:57:52.890
He does?

1179
00:57:53.050 --> 00:57:54.010
Oh, my gosh.

1180
00:57:54.030 --> 00:57:55.710
Have you not heard him go off?

1181
00:57:56.050 --> 00:57:57.730
No, I—okay, wait.

1182
00:57:57.930 --> 00:58:01.330
This is so funny, because I know what you're talking about.

1183
00:58:01.550 --> 00:58:06.070
Yes, both him and also Cate Blanchett.

1184
00:58:06.190 --> 00:58:11.050
Have you seen these compilations of them going on and on about the things that they hate?

1185
00:58:11.250 --> 00:58:13.070
She hates leaf blowers.

1186
00:58:13.150 --> 00:58:13.710
She does.

1187
00:58:13.910 --> 00:58:15.170
Like, so much she hates them.

1188
00:58:15.830 --> 00:58:18.530
I think you need to hear his hatred of backpacks, though.

1189
00:58:18.730 --> 00:58:21.910
Here's a clip of him talking about it on Late Night with Seth Meyers.

1190
00:58:22.330 --> 00:58:26.950
You are a man who—you said, I believe, you are a walking pet peeve,

1191
00:58:26.950 --> 00:58:29.450
in that you have a lot of pet peeves about human behavior.

1192
00:58:29.590 --> 00:58:30.790
Yeah, I'm an angry little man.

1193
00:58:30.950 --> 00:58:31.090
Yeah.

1194
00:58:31.830 --> 00:58:34.710
Can I read off some of your pet peeves and have you tee off?

1195
00:58:34.710 --> 00:58:36.250
You don't like people walking slowly.

1196
00:58:36.830 --> 00:58:37.630
I'm bearable!

1197
00:58:41.730 --> 00:58:42.050
Backpacks.

1198
00:58:42.110 --> 00:58:43.090
You don't care for backpacks.

1199
00:58:43.190 --> 00:58:43.430
Why not?

1200
00:58:43.470 --> 00:58:43.790
What's all that?

1201
00:58:43.850 --> 00:58:44.690
Why has everyone got a backpack?

1202
00:58:45.490 --> 00:58:46.150
Take it off.

1203
00:58:46.390 --> 00:58:47.070
Take it off.

1204
00:58:47.190 --> 00:58:48.830
But what if they have their stuff in it?

1205
00:58:49.050 --> 00:58:49.750
I don't care.

1206
00:58:49.930 --> 00:58:50.510
Carry it here.

1207
00:58:50.690 --> 00:58:50.950
Okay.

1208
00:58:50.950 --> 00:58:53.190
Oh, it's that—the bag's not an issue.

1209
00:58:53.230 --> 00:58:54.110
It's that it's on your back.

1210
00:58:54.230 --> 00:58:58.230
Well, I use the subway, the tube in London, quite a lot,

1211
00:58:58.270 --> 00:59:00.090
because you can't get it around any other way.

1212
00:59:00.270 --> 00:59:04.790
And there's always someone's huge backpack in my face with their stupid water bottle.

1213
00:59:06.010 --> 00:59:07.850
So you also don't care for water bottles?

1214
00:59:07.850 --> 00:59:08.130
What?

1215
00:59:08.170 --> 00:59:10.010
Why does everyone need a water bottle?

1216
00:59:11.610 --> 00:59:15.370
So, basically, I think Hugh Grant would really hate Ned's backpack.

1217
00:59:15.750 --> 00:59:16.670
Oh, for sure.

1218
00:59:16.790 --> 00:59:18.230
It took out a whole salad.

1219
00:59:18.550 --> 00:59:19.910
And he doesn't even notice.

1220
00:59:20.130 --> 00:59:20.530
I know.

1221
00:59:20.530 --> 00:59:21.290
All right.

1222
00:59:21.370 --> 00:59:26.230
Well, he's going to go upstairs to these offices, and he walks in,

1223
00:59:26.390 --> 00:59:29.050
and no one is expecting him.

1224
00:59:29.210 --> 00:59:32.430
He's just a guy with a backpack who's walked in.

1225
00:59:32.530 --> 00:59:33.810
He goes into the copy room.

1226
00:59:33.910 --> 00:59:35.850
He's kind of wandering around.

1227
00:59:35.850 --> 00:59:37.830
He's filming and taking pictures.

1228
00:59:37.830 --> 00:59:38.950
He's so excited.

1229
00:59:39.290 --> 00:59:40.830
He looks like a touriSt.

1230
00:59:41.310 --> 00:59:42.990
He looks out of place for sure.

1231
00:59:43.350 --> 00:59:49.130
So, Nicole clocks him, and she goes over, and she locks him inside of this copy room,

1232
00:59:49.170 --> 00:59:50.390
which is a glass room.

1233
00:59:50.530 --> 00:59:50.730
Yeah.

1234
00:59:50.730 --> 00:59:57.710
And now employees start wandering over, and then Ned is going to discover that he's been locked in the room.

1235
00:59:57.850 --> 01:00:00.210
And this whole exchange, it made me laugh very hard.

1236
01:00:00.210 --> 01:00:01.130
I think we need to hear it.

1237
01:00:01.810 --> 01:00:02.170
Hey, hey, hey, hey.

1238
01:00:02.230 --> 01:00:03.510
We do not have door money.

1239
01:00:03.550 --> 01:00:04.990
This is just a misunderstanding, okay?

1240
01:00:05.050 --> 01:00:07.510
I just, I start work at the paper.

1241
01:00:07.650 --> 01:00:08.170
Which paper?

1242
01:00:08.270 --> 01:00:08.930
News or toilet?

1243
01:00:09.450 --> 01:00:09.770
News.

1244
01:00:10.570 --> 01:00:12.830
Why would I say the paper about toilet paper?

1245
01:00:13.050 --> 01:00:14.030
We don't know you, sir.

1246
01:00:14.210 --> 01:00:15.790
How should we know why you say the things you say?

1247
01:00:15.890 --> 01:00:17.670
I've got a meeting at 2 o'clock, all right?

1248
01:00:17.670 --> 01:00:18.270
I'm just early.

1249
01:00:18.370 --> 01:00:18.990
That's all this is.

1250
01:00:19.150 --> 01:00:19.450
Okay.

1251
01:00:19.670 --> 01:00:20.410
Who's the meeting with?

1252
01:00:22.500 --> 01:00:24.060
Just briefly, I'm forgetting the name.

1253
01:00:25.580 --> 01:00:26.900
No, no, wait, wait, wait, wait.

1254
01:00:26.960 --> 01:00:27.920
It's something unique.

1255
01:00:28.220 --> 01:00:31.100
It's, is there a, is there a, a fan team that works here?

1256
01:00:31.740 --> 01:00:33.400
You'll find her in Les Mis.

1257
01:00:33.840 --> 01:00:34.460
All right, bye.

1258
01:00:36.560 --> 01:00:38.940
I really love Detrick in that scene.

1259
01:00:39.100 --> 01:00:39.540
Yes.

1260
01:00:39.740 --> 01:00:40.500
He's so great.

1261
01:00:40.600 --> 01:00:42.540
We don't know why you say the things you say, sir.

1262
01:00:42.940 --> 01:00:43.060
Yeah.

1263
01:00:43.540 --> 01:00:49.700
Well, Ned finally remembers Esmeralda's name at the same time that Mayer finally returns

1264
01:00:49.700 --> 01:00:52.700
and says, oh my gosh, I saw this guy downstairs.

1265
01:00:53.180 --> 01:00:54.560
He was talking to the camera crew.

1266
01:00:54.680 --> 01:00:55.360
He's legit.

1267
01:00:55.580 --> 01:00:55.940
Yes.

1268
01:00:56.120 --> 01:00:57.120
They let him out.

1269
01:00:57.380 --> 01:01:00.460
I want to point out at 14 minutes and 49 seconds,

1270
01:01:00.460 --> 01:01:05.560
we see behind Ned in the copy room on the side of a filing cabinet,

1271
01:01:06.140 --> 01:01:11.060
the same yellow froggy 101 radio sticker that was on the set of The Office.

1272
01:01:11.600 --> 01:01:13.180
Oh my gosh.

1273
01:01:13.560 --> 01:01:13.900
Yes.

1274
01:01:14.180 --> 01:01:15.520
Little Easter egg.

1275
01:01:15.880 --> 01:01:20.220
Well, I spotted an Easter egg on Oscar's desk that we're going to talk about later.

1276
01:01:20.400 --> 01:01:20.720
Yes.

1277
01:01:20.860 --> 01:01:21.940
And I have a clip.

1278
01:01:22.180 --> 01:01:25.100
Oh, well, it's making me wonder if there are more of these.

1279
01:01:25.260 --> 01:01:28.540
Like if I watch it again, will I see more of them in the background?

1280
01:01:28.800 --> 01:01:29.520
I think so.

1281
01:01:29.520 --> 01:01:31.120
I think they're hidden all over.

1282
01:01:31.420 --> 01:01:33.600
You think there's a Simpsons thing somewhere?

1283
01:01:33.860 --> 01:01:35.020
I would think so.

1284
01:01:35.120 --> 01:01:35.360
Right?

1285
01:01:35.540 --> 01:01:36.420
I looked for it.

1286
01:01:36.740 --> 01:01:38.400
If I missed it, let me know.

1287
01:01:38.540 --> 01:01:39.280
Or a ducky?

1288
01:01:39.760 --> 01:01:40.560
I don't know.

1289
01:01:40.620 --> 01:01:43.100
I did see a stuffed animal in a filing cabinet.

1290
01:01:43.560 --> 01:01:45.280
I couldn't really make out what it was.

1291
01:01:45.940 --> 01:01:46.420
Okay.

1292
01:01:46.420 --> 01:01:47.660
For future viewings.

1293
01:01:47.700 --> 01:01:48.060
Mm-hmm.

1294
01:01:49.020 --> 01:01:54.400
So when they let Ned out of the conference room, everyone's going to kind of come up and introduce

1295
01:01:54.400 --> 01:01:54.820
him.

1296
01:01:54.960 --> 01:02:02.480
But my favorite is when Travis, the softy toilet paper guy, shakes his hand and then turns it

1297
01:02:02.480 --> 01:02:04.520
into like a pat down.

1298
01:02:04.680 --> 01:02:05.100
Yes.

1299
01:02:05.260 --> 01:02:06.160
Just to make sure.

1300
01:02:06.260 --> 01:02:08.320
I love the character of Travis.

1301
01:02:08.740 --> 01:02:10.960
We have watched a few upcoming episodes.

1302
01:02:11.280 --> 01:02:14.580
There are a few scenes that he's in that crack me up.

1303
01:02:15.160 --> 01:02:16.660
Eric, you are hilarious.

1304
01:02:16.660 --> 01:02:17.300
Yes.

1305
01:02:17.480 --> 01:02:21.680
He's played by Eric Rahill, and he is our final writer-performer.

1306
01:02:21.940 --> 01:02:23.780
Oh, he is so funny.

1307
01:02:24.020 --> 01:02:24.420
Yes.

1308
01:02:24.700 --> 01:02:26.740
So Ned explains he's a new employee.

1309
01:02:26.960 --> 01:02:30.760
Mare is immediately worried that she's fired because she knows we don't have room for new

1310
01:02:30.760 --> 01:02:31.240
employees.

1311
01:02:31.640 --> 01:02:34.640
They get to chatting, and he's like, I'm a little shaken up.

1312
01:02:34.720 --> 01:02:37.380
I could use a bite to eat before my meeting.

1313
01:02:37.840 --> 01:02:39.300
I owe you a salad.

1314
01:02:39.560 --> 01:02:41.020
You want to get something to eat?

1315
01:02:41.100 --> 01:02:41.820
She's like, sure.

1316
01:02:42.080 --> 01:02:44.280
They now go down to a little cafe.

1317
01:02:44.280 --> 01:02:46.980
Hey, it looks like it's maybe somewhere in the building, right?

1318
01:02:47.120 --> 01:02:48.920
Like on the mezzanine level of the building?

1319
01:02:49.080 --> 01:02:49.380
Yes.

1320
01:02:49.620 --> 01:02:50.980
It's called Eat House.

1321
01:02:51.560 --> 01:02:51.900
Mm-hmm.

1322
01:02:52.080 --> 01:02:52.500
Get it?

1323
01:02:52.680 --> 01:02:53.160
Eat House.

1324
01:02:53.160 --> 01:02:53.540
Eat House.

1325
01:02:53.740 --> 01:02:53.940
Mm-hmm.

1326
01:02:54.040 --> 01:02:54.240
Yeah.

1327
01:02:54.760 --> 01:02:58.020
And this is where the two of them start to get to know each other.

1328
01:02:58.940 --> 01:03:00.280
It's such a lovely scene.

1329
01:03:00.320 --> 01:03:01.840
I love Chelsea in this scene.

1330
01:03:01.980 --> 01:03:02.100
Yeah.

1331
01:03:02.100 --> 01:03:02.720
I love her.

1332
01:03:02.900 --> 01:03:10.880
I have to imagine that when Greg was watching her in this scene, he was dazzled because she's

1333
01:03:10.880 --> 01:03:11.220
incredible.

1334
01:03:11.620 --> 01:03:12.680
She's so great in it.

1335
01:03:12.780 --> 01:03:13.580
I did have questions.

1336
01:03:13.580 --> 01:03:17.960
This is when I wish we had the script because Ned does this thing where he's picking out

1337
01:03:17.960 --> 01:03:20.360
all of the tomatoes out of his salad.

1338
01:03:20.700 --> 01:03:21.060
Yes.

1339
01:03:21.140 --> 01:03:25.380
He's like talking about his backstory, where he came from, but all the while he's picking

1340
01:03:25.380 --> 01:03:26.040
at his salad.

1341
01:03:26.780 --> 01:03:28.560
Like separating things out of it.

1342
01:03:28.660 --> 01:03:28.960
Yes.

1343
01:03:29.120 --> 01:03:31.480
And she becomes very sort of fixated on that.

1344
01:03:31.560 --> 01:03:32.440
She's like, what are you doing?

1345
01:03:32.440 --> 01:03:34.960
And I was like, that had to have been in the script.

1346
01:03:35.180 --> 01:03:36.540
Like he didn't just do that, right?

1347
01:03:36.540 --> 01:03:43.080
I don't think so because he has a very funny line where he says, oh, this is the pile of

1348
01:03:43.080 --> 01:03:45.160
things I don't want to eat from the salad.

1349
01:03:45.300 --> 01:03:47.220
This is the pile of eat-alones.

1350
01:03:47.440 --> 01:03:47.740
Yes.

1351
01:03:47.740 --> 01:03:48.900
And then this is the salad.

1352
01:03:49.080 --> 01:03:49.540
That's right.

1353
01:03:49.600 --> 01:03:50.040
That's right.

1354
01:03:50.580 --> 01:03:52.100
I just love that writing.

1355
01:03:52.320 --> 01:03:56.880
But what I do wonder is, is there someone in the writer's room who does that to their

1356
01:03:56.880 --> 01:03:57.280
salad?

1357
01:03:57.280 --> 01:03:58.200
I'm sure.

1358
01:03:58.400 --> 01:04:00.640
You know all these things make their way in.

1359
01:04:00.760 --> 01:04:01.340
That's right.

1360
01:04:01.720 --> 01:04:05.880
So we find out that Ned started by working at his father's cardboard company.

1361
01:04:06.100 --> 01:04:10.880
Then through a connection that his dad had at Enervate, he ends up working at Softies in

1362
01:04:10.880 --> 01:04:11.360
Chicago.

1363
01:04:11.660 --> 01:04:16.220
And now, per his request, he's been moved to Toledo to work at the newspaper.

1364
01:04:16.480 --> 01:04:18.920
We also learn that he's a very good salesman.

1365
01:04:19.500 --> 01:04:20.020
Yes.

1366
01:04:20.240 --> 01:04:24.360
And we learn a little bit about his vision for what he wants to do here.

1367
01:04:25.100 --> 01:04:28.380
We're now going to cut back to the offices where some people are talking.

1368
01:04:28.840 --> 01:04:33.080
There's a hilarious conversation between Oscar and Travis where Travis is sort of telling

1369
01:04:33.080 --> 01:04:39.040
him how to behave if you ever hit a deer with your car, what to do with your car, to avoid

1370
01:04:39.040 --> 01:04:39.780
hitting the deer.

1371
01:04:40.060 --> 01:04:41.520
This scene was so funny.

1372
01:04:41.520 --> 01:04:47.200
And this is a scene that I think might have gotten cut if you only had 21 minutes.

1373
01:04:47.360 --> 01:04:47.700
Yes.

1374
01:04:47.900 --> 01:04:52.560
Because there's no purpose for this other than to just be very, very funny, to just watch

1375
01:04:52.560 --> 01:04:54.240
two funny people in a funny scene.

1376
01:04:54.360 --> 01:04:58.160
It does have a moment, though, that furthers the storyline that Oscar doesn't want to

1377
01:04:58.160 --> 01:05:02.900
be in it because when Oscar sees the camera, he kind of scooches behind his computer monitor.

1378
01:05:03.080 --> 01:05:03.800
Yes, that's true.

1379
01:05:04.000 --> 01:05:05.540
So that does serve that purpose.

1380
01:05:05.860 --> 01:05:10.900
But I couldn't help but think that Travis and Dwight would get along really well.

1381
01:05:11.000 --> 01:05:12.740
Definitely based on this speech.

1382
01:05:12.860 --> 01:05:13.200
Mm-hmm.

1383
01:05:13.600 --> 01:05:14.520
And the pat down.

1384
01:05:14.920 --> 01:05:16.140
And the pat down.

1385
01:05:16.140 --> 01:05:16.760
You're right.

1386
01:05:17.360 --> 01:05:22.420
I do want to give a quick shout out to Scott McGinnis, who is the prop master for the paper.

1387
01:05:22.640 --> 01:05:26.440
We chatted with him when we visited the set and we exchanged emails.

1388
01:05:26.720 --> 01:05:31.940
He shared with us that he knew Phil Shea well, and he did a lot of the same things for the

1389
01:05:31.940 --> 01:05:37.400
paper, like reaching out to local businesses for menus, magnets, et cetera, that Phil did

1390
01:05:37.400 --> 01:05:39.880
for the office with the local Scranton businesses.

1391
01:05:40.320 --> 01:05:44.300
And I asked Scott what it was like to work on the paper, and he said, is it possible to

1392
01:05:44.300 --> 01:05:46.220
work with Greg Daniels without a story?

1393
01:05:46.420 --> 01:05:47.300
He's wonderful.

1394
01:05:48.840 --> 01:05:52.740
And I did ask him if we ever get to see that bird, and he said yes.

1395
01:05:53.080 --> 01:05:55.580
Oh, got some inside scoop.

1396
01:05:55.920 --> 01:05:56.140
Yeah.

1397
01:05:57.560 --> 01:06:04.360
Well, now we're going to have a talking head by the character Adalola, and she's telling

1398
01:06:04.360 --> 01:06:06.660
us about her deskmate, Adam.

1399
01:06:07.020 --> 01:06:08.480
He has four kids.

1400
01:06:09.140 --> 01:06:11.500
His wife makes him the same lunch every day.

1401
01:06:12.100 --> 01:06:15.000
He basically eats whatever the kids don't want to eat.

1402
01:06:15.520 --> 01:06:19.040
And I guess you could say I'm not sure she thinks very highly of him.

1403
01:06:19.340 --> 01:06:24.400
While she's saying all of this, it's so hilarious to me because they cut to Adam.

1404
01:06:24.400 --> 01:06:27.960
He is drinking out of a dinosaur sippy cup.

1405
01:06:28.380 --> 01:06:30.820
He's like literally eating a kid's lunch.

1406
01:06:31.100 --> 01:06:32.140
It's really funny.

1407
01:06:32.480 --> 01:06:37.760
We see a little bit of both of their desks in this montage, and I thought I'd share with

1408
01:06:37.760 --> 01:06:37.960
you.

1409
01:06:38.420 --> 01:06:42.380
First of all, Adalola's desk, she's got a lot on her desk.

1410
01:06:42.660 --> 01:06:44.980
She's got a lot of papers, a lot of things.

1411
01:06:45.100 --> 01:06:45.960
It's a little messy.

1412
01:06:46.280 --> 01:06:51.400
But I think she's where people go to get little treats because on the front of her desk,

1413
01:06:51.540 --> 01:06:53.360
she has glass canisters of candy.

1414
01:06:53.360 --> 01:06:53.940
Oh!

1415
01:06:54.340 --> 01:06:56.600
Kind of reminded me of Pam's desk a little bit.

1416
01:06:56.800 --> 01:06:57.180
Okay, okay.

1417
01:06:58.380 --> 01:07:03.360
Adam's desk is the one I was talking about, like Esmeralda's.

1418
01:07:03.780 --> 01:07:07.140
Holy moly, how many more things could you fit on this desk?

1419
01:07:07.320 --> 01:07:09.220
It is wall-to-wall things.

1420
01:07:09.560 --> 01:07:14.160
And when we visited the set, we actually talked to Alex while he was at his desk,

1421
01:07:14.220 --> 01:07:16.180
and he described it a little bit for us.

1422
01:07:17.440 --> 01:07:21.040
I have various books that you can only see the spines of.

1423
01:07:21.100 --> 01:07:24.660
This is a book by Ken Coapes, who directed the second episode.

1424
01:07:25.100 --> 01:07:28.020
And it's facing in, so you can't see the spine, but it says,

1425
01:07:28.140 --> 01:07:32.160
but what I really want to do is direct, which is what Ken gave me in the book.

1426
01:07:32.520 --> 01:07:35.980
This is a book about one of my mentors in my job before comedy.

1427
01:07:36.360 --> 01:07:38.540
A guy named Larry Lucchino used to run the Red Sox.

1428
01:07:38.660 --> 01:07:39.880
The book's called Masters of the Game.

1429
01:07:39.880 --> 01:07:45.740
And so, yeah, because my character is a dad, he's got a bunch of, like,

1430
01:07:46.180 --> 01:07:48.240
he can't play at home, so I've got a bunch of toys,

1431
01:07:48.260 --> 01:07:53.620
like these Velcro ball catchers, like Rubik's Cubes, like this fart gun.

1432
01:07:55.480 --> 01:07:57.140
Yeah, it does different farts.

1433
01:07:57.220 --> 01:07:58.020
I have a question.

1434
01:07:58.280 --> 01:07:58.560
Yeah.

1435
01:07:58.780 --> 01:08:02.500
I have a question about this container of toothpick cleaners.

1436
01:08:02.900 --> 01:08:03.360
Floss picks.

1437
01:08:03.960 --> 01:08:05.900
Is that props or is that you?

1438
01:08:06.080 --> 01:08:07.200
Oh, that's props.

1439
01:08:07.200 --> 01:08:10.320
The only, the stuff that's made, but I love all of the props.

1440
01:08:10.560 --> 01:08:11.840
This is a mini bop it.

1441
01:08:12.120 --> 01:08:12.560
Oh, yeah.

1442
01:08:12.580 --> 01:08:15.880
I'm really, I'm really good at mini bop it,

1443
01:08:15.960 --> 01:08:18.580
but once, like, in an early episode at, like, a 6 a.m.

1444
01:08:18.660 --> 01:08:19.100
call time,

1445
01:08:19.260 --> 01:08:22.660
I was, like, doing the bop it, like, in a bit of a trance state,

1446
01:08:22.700 --> 01:08:25.580
and I looked up and everyone was, like, watching me.

1447
01:08:26.420 --> 01:08:28.040
It was the wrong kind of attention.

1448
01:08:29.140 --> 01:08:31.860
I have a question about this Boston snow globe.

1449
01:08:32.080 --> 01:08:35.360
Is your character, does your character have a Boston backstory?

1450
01:08:35.360 --> 01:08:38.720
My character, like me, does have a Boston backstory.

1451
01:08:38.960 --> 01:08:41.920
I'm from, I'm a proud Brookline, Massachusettsan.

1452
01:08:42.860 --> 01:08:45.900
Not a Newtonian, like Novak and Krasinski,

1453
01:08:47.240 --> 01:08:51.400
but it's a bit of a tribute to all of the Bostonians

1454
01:08:51.400 --> 01:08:52.520
who have come through the show.

1455
01:08:53.040 --> 01:08:57.960
And, yeah, my character is from Boston originally,

1456
01:08:58.200 --> 01:08:59.200
although it's never come up.

1457
01:08:59.960 --> 01:09:04.600
I see that you also have the Magic 8-Ball on your desk,

1458
01:09:04.780 --> 01:09:07.820
which was in Michael Scott's office,

1459
01:09:08.120 --> 01:09:12.360
and a Magic 8-Ball seems to find its way onto a desk

1460
01:09:12.360 --> 01:09:14.180
in every iteration of the office.

1461
01:09:14.400 --> 01:09:15.840
So, you know, that wasn't my choice,

1462
01:09:15.920 --> 01:09:17.100
but when I noticed it,

1463
01:09:17.720 --> 01:09:20.120
I kept hoping that no one would call me on it

1464
01:09:20.120 --> 01:09:21.980
because I really wanted to keep it

1465
01:09:21.980 --> 01:09:24.260
because I was such a big fan of the original show.

1466
01:09:24.860 --> 01:09:32.000
And so I thought, oh, man, Oscar has his little nod to Michael Scott.

1467
01:09:32.320 --> 01:09:35.900
I love that I get to have a little tiny nod to Michael Scott, too.

1468
01:09:36.000 --> 01:09:36.720
But there are so many.

1469
01:09:36.840 --> 01:09:38.900
Yes, I have a bevy of chapsticks.

1470
01:09:39.660 --> 01:09:40.820
I have lots of chapsticks.

1471
01:09:40.960 --> 01:09:44.760
And this is a ball signed by a bunch of the crew,

1472
01:09:44.980 --> 01:09:48.180
which is a really nice, nice thing to have.

1473
01:09:48.760 --> 01:09:50.340
And, yeah, there are lots of little nods

1474
01:09:50.340 --> 01:09:51.300
to the crew around the office.

1475
01:09:51.300 --> 01:09:55.640
A lot of the employees of the month are the, yeah, a lot of the plaques.

1476
01:09:55.760 --> 01:09:57.460
And, like, it's a really fun set.

1477
01:09:58.580 --> 01:09:59.500
Isn't that great?

1478
01:09:59.680 --> 01:09:59.900
Yeah.

1479
01:10:00.120 --> 01:10:02.160
He also went on to show us all the pictures

1480
01:10:02.160 --> 01:10:04.160
that are, quote, unquote, all of his mini kids.

1481
01:10:04.380 --> 01:10:06.780
And they're just all of his friends' kids.

1482
01:10:07.000 --> 01:10:07.220
Yeah.

1483
01:10:07.220 --> 01:10:09.380
Like, people that he holds dear to his heart.

1484
01:10:09.800 --> 01:10:11.500
But his desk had so many toys.

1485
01:10:11.600 --> 01:10:14.640
It had a marble racer, had all these things.

1486
01:10:14.660 --> 01:10:18.440
But I loved hearing how there was a little bit of things from his life,

1487
01:10:18.440 --> 01:10:21.880
plus things that are props, plus nods to the office.

1488
01:10:22.160 --> 01:10:25.520
And I also just love how they continued the tradition,

1489
01:10:25.520 --> 01:10:26.860
like we had on the office,

1490
01:10:26.980 --> 01:10:30.340
of having crew featured in all these little places on plaques and things.

1491
01:10:30.460 --> 01:10:31.300
I love that, too.

1492
01:10:32.060 --> 01:10:33.900
Well, now we're going back to lunch.

1493
